 Receives webhooks on `POST /hooks/:source`, checks the HMAC-SHA256 signature, stores the delivery and forwards the body to the source's target URL.
 
-Accepted webhooks return `202` with a `pending` status after the delivery is saved in Postgres. A background worker forwards them. Target responses with status 500–599 are retried with exponential backoff (100, 200, 400, then 800 ms), up to five total attempts per delivery. The first attempt is immediate, and the backoff follows the saved attempt count across restarts. Other responses and network errors are not retried. Each attempt uses the same body and `x-relay-delivery` ID.
+Accepted webhooks return `202` with a `pending` status after the delivery is saved in Postgres. A background worker forwards them. Target responses with status 500–599 are retried with exponential backoff (100, 200, 400, then 800 ms), up to five total attempts per delivery. The first attempt is immediate, and the backoff follows the saved attempt count across restarts. Queued retries use Postgres availability times instead of sleeping while holding a lease. Other responses and network errors are not retried. Each attempt uses the same body and `x-relay-delivery` ID.
 
 Set `DATABASE_URL` to the Postgres connection URL (otherwise the standard `PG*` environment variables apply). Apply `migrations/001_deliveries.sql` and then `migrations/002_attempt_history.sql` before starting the app (existing installations only need the second migration): `psql "$DATABASE_URL" -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f migrations/001_deliveries.sql -f migrations/002_attempt_history.sql`. The `deliveries` table stores delivery bodies, status, attempt counts, and pending work in a single row; use durable Postgres storage. Concurrent workers claim rows atomically with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`. Pending work resumes automatically on application restart. Claimed work becomes available again after a 60-second lease; each outbound request has a 30-second timeout.
 
