 `GET /admin/deliveries` returns an array of delivery metadata; `GET /admin/deliveries/:id` returns one record or `404` if absent. Both expose only `id`, `source`, `target`, `receivedAt` (Unix milliseconds), `status`, and `attempts`. Payload bodies are never included, including in errors. These routes have no built-in authentication; restrict access through your deployment's admin access controls.
 
-`GET /admin/deliveries/:id/attempts` returns an array in attempt order containing `attempt` (one-based), `startedAt` and `completedAt` (Unix milliseconds), `statusCode`, and `outcome` (`started`, `retry`, `delivered`, or `failed`). An unfinished attempt has a null completion time and status code; network failures also have a null status code. The start is recorded before backoff and sending. No request/response bodies or raw errors are included. Existing deliveries without recorded history return `[]`; unknown IDs return `404` and storage failures return a generic `503`. History persists alongside the attempt count in Postgres, including unfinished attempts after interruption.
+`GET /admin/deliveries/:id/attempts` returns an array in attempt order containing `attempt` (one-based), `startedAt` and `completedAt` (Unix milliseconds), `statusCode`, and `outcome` (`started`, `retry`, `delivered`, or `failed`). An unfinished attempt has a null completion time and status code; network failures also have a null status code. The start is recorded before sending (and before backoff for inline delivery). No request/response bodies or raw errors are included. Existing deliveries without recorded history return `[]`; unknown IDs return `404` and storage failures return a generic `503`. History persists alongside the attempt count in Postgres, including unfinished attempts after interruption.
 
 Sources are configured with the `SOURCES` env var, a JSON object:
 
 ```json
-{ "github": { "secret": "dev-secret", "target": "http://localhost:4000/in" } }
+{ "github": { "secret": "dev-secret", "target": "http://localhost:4000/in", "retryDelaysMs": [1000, 2000, 5000, 10000] } }
 ```
 
+`retryDelaysMs` is optional per source endpoint. Supply exactly four integer delays in milliseconds (0–2147483647), one before each retry; omission keeps 100/200/400/800 ms. Invalid schedules fail startup. The attempt limit and retry eligibility are unchanged. The worker uses the current source configuration when scheduling each next retry, including for existing deliveries; already scheduled availability times remain unchanged. No database migration is needed for this setting.
